{
  "figure": "fig_ex06_block_threshold",
  "caption": "The conduction-block threshold is a curve in the (L_gap, rho) plane, not a single critical coupling ratio: a single-node gap blocks only below rho = 0.0670, rising 135 % to rho = 0.1575 and then saturating beyond about 0.1 cm. The saturation is itself informative -- once the gap is longer than the distance over which failure develops, whether the wave survives depends on the local coupling alone, not on how far it must travel. Panel (c) shows the numerical-choice sensitivity is not merely quantitative: the arithmetic mean shifts the threshold by up to 4.2 %, and for a single-node gap it cannot produce block at ANY coupling ratio, because its interface conductance tends to D0/2 rather than to zero as rho falls. Panel (d) shows why the criterion needs a 0.3 cm margin -- charge leaks passively past a blocked gap and decays over about one space constant without ever becoming a propagating front.",
